    "title": "Easter 2026: Meaning, history, Holy Week dates and end of Lent",
    "text": "The Resurrection, ca 1475. Found in the collection of the Museu Nacional d'Art de Catalunya, Barcelona.Fine Art Images/Heritage Images/Getty ImagesEaster Sunday has arrived. Here’s what to know about the religious holiday, including its history and why it’s celebrated today.When is Easter 2026?Dates vary each year based on the lunar calendar. Easter falls on the first Sunday after the Paschal full moon, which occurs on or after the spring equinox. This year, the spring equinox was on March 20, and Easter is April 5.Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢What is the origin of Easter? Here's the history For Christians, Easter commemorates the resurrection of Christ, a fundamental belief in Christianity. According to the Gospels, Christ was arrested, tried and sentenced to death by crucifixion. Christians believe he died on the cross on Good Friday, was buried in a tomb and rose from the dead three days later on Easter Sunday.Christ’s resurrection is considered to be a victory over death and sin. Christians believe this validates Christ’s identity as the Son of God and symbolizes the forgiveness of sin. Spiritual teachings claim Christ paid the price for humanity’s sins, offering believers the promise of eternal life and resurrection.Easter Sunday is also sometimes called Resurrection Sunday.Article continues below this adHoly Week: Dates and meaningsSaint Seine church, Stained glass, Passion of Christ, The crucifixion, The Last supper, Corbigny, France.Godong/Universal Images Group via Getty ImagesHoly Week began with Palm Sunday on March 29 and ends with Easter Sunday on April 5. It is a sacred week for Christians, marked by observances, traditions and celebrations honoring Christ’s final days on Earth.Article continues below this adHere’s a breakdown of each day of Holy Week and its significance. Palm Sunday, March 29: Marks Jesus’ entry into Jerusalem, where crowds welcomed him with palm branches. Maundy Thursday, April 2: Commemorates the Last Supper and Jesus washing his disciples’ feet. Good Friday, April 3: Marks the crucifixion and death of Jesus. Holy Saturday, April 4: Represents the day Jesus' body rested in the tomb. Easter Sunday, April 5: Celebrates the resurrection of Jesus. What is Lent? When does Lent end?Lent is a 40-day period of fasting, prayer, and reflection observed by many Christians in preparation for Easter. This represents the 40 days that Jesus spent fasting and praying in the desert before starting his public ministry.Article continues below this adDuring Lent, many believers give up certain luxuries — such as sweets, specific foods, social media or alcohol — as a form of self-discipline. Others take on new spiritual practices, like daily prayer or acts of charity. Sundays are not traditionally counted in the 40 days, as they are considered days of celebration rather than fasting.Lent always begins on Ash Wednesday, which was February 18 this year, and ends on Maundy Thursday, which was April 2.Stories you won't want to miss, handpicked by Statesman editors.April 5, 2026Alexis Simmerman has been a trending reporter for the Austin American-Statesman since January 2024. She is a 2023 graduate of the Missouri School of Journalism and has a Bachelor of Arts in English from the University of Missouri.",
